  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Leccinum insigne, commonly known as the aspen bolete, is a medium-sized fungus in the bolete family characterized by its distinctive appearance and association with aspen trees. This edible species is popular among foragers in New England and other northern regions where aspen grows abundantly.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap color and texture",
      "description": "Reddish-brown to orange-brown cap with a slightly felty or velvety surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem scabrosity",
      "description": "Elongated stem with distinctive dark brown to blackish scales or granules on a lighter background, particularly visible on the lower half",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore surface",
      "description": "Yellow to olive-yellow pores on the underside that may bruise greenish or blue-gray when handled",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Leccinum insigne grows in association with aspen and poplar trees throughout New England, preferring moist woodlands and forest edges from summer through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'insigne' means 'remarkable' or 'distinguished,' referring to the bolete's striking appearance, and the scaly stem pattern helps it stand out from other similar bolete species in the genus Leccinum.",
